Doing is more than announcing a plan
Implementation can include:

rule-making and policy;
habitat restoration;
public involvement;
law enforcement;
surveys; and
day-to-day professional decisions.


Time: 5 minutes. Source: Chapter 6, §6.3.3. Explain that implementation occurs within legal mandates, agency missions, capacity, and constraints.


Source: Chapter 6, §6.3.3

Era of Exploitation: short-term use, lasting damage


Conditions

few regulatory frameworks;
industrial-scale markets;
new harvest technologies; and
westward expansion.


Consequences

depleted deer and beaver;
reduced predator ranges;
declining salmon; and
near extermination of bison.



Source: Chapter 2, §2.2.3 and Figure 2.2.


Time: 3 minutes. Connect conditions to outcomes. The American Fisheries Society was founded in 1870 during this era.

Effective management has a wider standard
Effective management and conservation must be:

biologically sound;
socially informed;
publicly defensible;
ethically grounded; and
practical to implement on the ground.


Source: Chapter 1, §1.5.


Time: 2 minutes. Return to the opening question. Human dimensions is woven into daily work—from defining a problem to implementing a decision—not a separate final step.
